<<<
+ nunciations é’ksplitiv, éksplé’tiv, in that order.
===
+ nunciations ĕ'ksplĭtĭv, ĕksplē'tĭv, in that order.
>>>

<<<
for DIFFERENTIATION; cf. expert, & see Noun & ADJECTIVE ACCENT; the noun would be é’ksplitiv.
===
for ^^DIFFERENTIATION^^; cf. *expert*, & see ^^NOUN & ADJECTIVE ACCENT^^; the noun would be ĕ'ksplĭtĭv.
>>>

<<<
explicit & express, With a certain class of nouns (e.g. declaration, testimony, promise, contract, understanding, incitement, prohibition), either adjective
===
**explicit & express.** With a certain class of nouns (e.g. *declaration, testimony, promise, contract, understanding, incitement, prohibition*), either adjective
>>>

<<<
with explicit, but has come into it by accident. An express promise
===
with *explicit*, but has come into it by accident. An express promise
>>>

<<<
promise, i.e. one put into words, express being a Latin participle of the kind seen in suspect = suspected, subject = subjected, & many others.
===
promise, i.e. one put into words, *express* being a Latin participle of the kind seen in *suspect* = suspected, *subject* = subjected, & many others.
>>>

<<<
the familiar adjectival sense (for the express purpose of; express malice is when one with a sedate deliberate mind & formed design doth . . .) should influence its meaning; the idea of special intention is now almost invariably distinctive of egpress when it is preferred to explicit.
===
the familiar adjectival sense (*for the express purpose of; express malice is when one with a sedate deliberate mind & formed design doth* . . .) should influence its meaning; the idea of special intention is now almost invariably distinctive of *express* when it is preferred to *explicit.*
>>>

<<<
exploit. See Noun & VERB ACCENT.
===
**exploit.** See ^^NOUN & VERB ACCENT^^.
>>>

