<<<
exposé. See FRENCH WORDS.
===
**exposé.** See ^^FRENCH WORDS^^.
>>>

<<<
exposition in the sense public show of goods &c. is a GALLICISM (or Americanism) for exhibition.
===
**exposition** in the sense *public show of goods* &c. is a ^^GALLICISM^^ (or Americanism) for *exhibition.*
>>>

<<<
ex post facto. This is the established spelling; but the person who knows the Latin words is worse off with it in this disguise than one who does not; it should be ex postfacto (ex on the footing of postfacto later enactment).
===
**ex post facto.** This is the established spelling; but the person who knows the Latin words is worse off with it in this disguise than one who does not; it should be *ex postfacto* (*ex* on the footing of *postfacto* later enactment).
>>>

<<<
The ordinary rule of HyrnHens would then be applied, & we should say It is undesirable to legislate ex postfacto, but ex-postfacto legislation is undesirable.
===
The ordinary rule of ^^HYPHENS^^ would then be applied, & we should say *It is undesirable to legislate ex postfacto,* but *ex-postfacto legislation is undesirable.*
>>>

<<<
E. legislation is, for instance, the making of an act illegal after it has been committed; but what is referred to in facto is not the ‘doing’ of the action but the ‘ enacting ’ of the law.
===
*E.* legislation is, for instance, the making of an act illegal after it has been committed; but what is referred to in *facto* is not the 'doing' of the action but the 'enacting' of the law.
>>>

<<<
See EXPLICIT.
===
**express, adj.** See ^^EXPLICIT^^.
>>>

<<<
express, vb. Mr Justice Sankey expressed himself much troubled by the views expressed in Lord Wrenbury’s letter./Both men afterwards expressed themselves perfectly satisfied.
===
**express,** vb. *Mr Justice Sankey expressed himself much troubled by the views expressed in Lord Wrenbury's letter.*/*Both men afterwards expressed themselves perfectly satisfied.*
>>>

<<<
Insert as after himself & themselves.
===
Insert *as* after *himself* & *themselves.*
>>>

<<<
support is needed for the use with as.
===
support is needed for the use with *as.*
>>>

<<<
The fact is that ANALocy is being allowed to confuse eapress with declare just as regard is wrongly given the construction of consider.
===
The fact is that ^^ANALOGY^^ is being allowed to confuse *express* with *declare* just as *regard* is wrongly given the construction of *consider.*
>>>

<<<
expressive. For‘ to use ane. word” see SUPERIORITY.
===
**expressive.** For 'to use an e. word' see ^^SUPERIORITY^^.
>>>

<<<
expurgate. See -ATABLE.
===
**expurgate.** See ^^-ATABLE^^.
>>>

<<<
extant had formerly the same sense as existent or existing, & was as widely applicable.
===
**extant** had formerly the same sense as *existent* or *existing,* & was as widely applicable.
>>>

<<<
Its sense & its application have been narrowed till it means only ‘ still in existence or not having perished at the present or the given past or future time’, & is applied almost exclusively to documents, buildings or monuments, & customs.
===
Its sense & its application have been narrowed till it means only 'still in existence or not having perished at the present or the given past or future time', & is applied almost exclusively to documents, buildings or monuments, & customs.
>>>

